 | `seedbed_probe` | **Can a seed start on what a dead plant leaves behind?** N seeds sown onto a mat of one material laid across the lab bed, against bare soil, counting germinations | Built for the owner's report that *"the ground gets covered in deadwood and new seeds cannot germinate in deadwood and everything dies"*. **It reproduces**: 16 of 16 seeds germinate on bare soil and **0 of 16** on a deadwood mat. **Two of its columns exist because the first version was wrong in both, and both errors generalise.** *`estab` counts organisms with more than one cell*, because an ungerminated seed **is** a live organism with one cell -- the first run reported 168 "plants" on bare soil of which 143 were seeds that had done nothing. And *`resting on` names the material under every surviving seed*, which is the scene check `CLAUDE.md` demands: a patch-width mat scored as permeable because seeds are `Powder` and **roll off it** onto the bare soil beside it, so the mat arm was not measuring the mat. **The mat-standing triple (laid -> at sowing -> at end) is the column that carries the finding** and is the one to copy into any probe over a decaying obstacle: litter reads a clean 16/16 and is *not* permeable -- 71% of its mat had rotted before the seeds landed (1,506 -> 436), while deadwood's was 85% intact (1,506 -> 1,285). Without that triple the two are indistinguishable and the wrong material gets blamed. **Its sharpest result is a negative one**: a mat of *soil* -- penetrable, water-holding, the thing one would convert debris into -- blocks just as totally (0/16, dry after 6,600 frames), so the gate is **water, not material**, and "turn the debris into soil" is measurably not a fix. Adding `water_capacity` and a low `penetration_resistance` to the debris does not fix it either (still 0/16, available water 0.000): fresh ground is created dry and does not wet from the damp soil below. Prints the species' root `penetration_force` beside each material's `penetration_resistance` and `water_capacity` -- the gate's inputs rather than its verdict -- so a null says *which* gate closed. Echoes its own parameters |
 | `soil_wetting_probe` | **Does fresh ground ever hold enough water to germinate a seed on it?** One bone-dry soil cell over field-capacity soil, read as `soil_moisture` and `plant_available_fraction` at fixed stops, against the species' own germination thresholds | Built to settle a contradiction between two documents rather than to explore: `seedbed_probe`'s entry here and [`where-a-dead-plant-goes-2026-08-31.md`](where-a-dead-plant-goes-2026-08-31.md) both say fresh ground *"is created dry and does not wet from the damp soil below"*, while `update.rs`'s capillary rule is gated on nothing but `water_capacity > 0` and a gap above the rest threshold -- and a dry cell against field capacity clears that by an order of magnitude. **Both are right, and the reconciliation is the finding**: fresh ground wets *fast* -- it crosses grass's `soil_water_threshold` (0.10) at **frame 20** and herb's (0.15) at **frame 24**, reaching 0.445 -- and then, under the **full** pipeline rather than the CA driver alone, evaporation takes it back through both thresholds by frame ~500-600 and it settles at exactly `SOIL_WILTING_POINT` -- **available water 0.000** -- by frame 3,000 and stays there for ever. So the reported number reproduces and its stated cause does not, which makes the two documents' claim *true-but-for-a-different-reason* and puts the real constraint somewhere else: **a sealed bench dries out, and a seed's chance to germinate on any given patch is a window that closes rather than a state.** That is why the arm matters more than the number here -- `parallel::step` alone holds 0.445 indefinitely and `frame::step` does not, so a probe run on the CA driver would have reported the opposite answer with equal confidence. Decay-created and hand-placed cells are **bit-identical** at every stop (nothing in the rule reads provenance), which is the control that says the decay path is not special. **Its scene error is the reusable half**: the first version read a uniform zero everywhere because a 1-cell-wide soil column standing in open air is not a stable scene -- `soil.ron`'s `friction_angle: 33` is nowhere near the 90-degree slope such a tower presents, so ordinary powder movement slumped it before capillary had anything to measure. Every column is walled in stone for its full height so the only thing that can move `aux` is the mechanism under test; copy that into any probe over a per-cell soil channel |
 | `reseed_probe` | **The whole reseeding funnel in the lab bed, stage by stage** — seeds borne, where each standing seed came to rest and *which* germination gate is shut on it, germinations, seedlings, established plants, and **how many of the bed's columns the stand actually holds** | Built for the owner's *"nothing spreads them"*, which is four claims about four stages that every existing harness collapses into one number. **`scatter=1` is its positive control and the point of the binary**: every seed the world creates is set down once on open ground at a uniformly random column, so dispersal limitation is gone and nothing else has changed -- it measures ~1.5x germination and ~1.3x coverage, i.e. dispersal is real and is not the headline. **`col=` exists because of a scene error that changes the answer**: `LabBox::spread(1)` puts a single founder at column 256, the darkest column in the bed, so a one-founder run is not a smaller eight-founder run -- 4 of 4 founders die there and 4 of 4 breed under a lamp. Prints the **bench light profile** every 8 columns, which is what found the 32-column dead bands. Reports the gate's *inputs* (resting material, its `water_capacity`, plant-available water, ambient light) against the species' own thresholds rather than re-deriving the verdict, and separates **on a seed pile** from **on plant tissue**, which are the owner's hypothesis and what is actually there. **Its `seeds borne` column is `World::seeds_borne` and had to be added to the engine**: two outside-in counts of the same thing are both keyed on the organism slot, which `push_organism` re-uses, so both miss the recycled births and *agree with each other* while doing so. Also prints the **bench light profile** every 8 columns, a per-frame cost line whose control arm is `founders=0` (no plants, so only the arm under test differs), and `World::germinations_in_place` -- germinations on an organism holding more than one cell, which a borne seed can never be, and which is what named `open-bugs-handoff.md` #Z4's mechanism. Echoes its own parameters. **`launch=N` is the 2026-09-03 addition and its seam matters more than its arm**: it sets `Behavior::Reproduce::seed_launch` -- zero in every shipped species -- through `SpeciesRegistry::set_param`, which writes one authored parameter by `ParamId` into the **live registry** rather than into a `.ron`. Every parameter sweep in this repo has until now had to edit a species file and rebuild, which is the `include_str!` trap that has produced whole invalid sweeps here (identical output across settings, because the prebuilt binary never read the file); a sweep through `set_param` cannot go stale, and it refuses outright when the write matches nothing. Measured on `herb`, 8 founders, 27,000 frames, three world seeds: a reach of 12 holds 500/465/481 columns of 512 against 398/454/396, and puts **91/92/88 plants more than fifteen columns from a founder against 66/69/61 -- +38%, up on 3 of 3 seeds** |
